One regex with ordered alternatives (escaped braces first, then placeholders, then any lone brace) drives re.sub with a callback, so every brace is classified exactly once in a left-to-right scan and replacements are never re-scanned.
```python
import re


def render_brace_template(template: str, values: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    token = re.compile(r"\{\{|\}\}|\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\}|[{}]")

    def replace(m: re.Match) -> str:
        text = m.group(0)
        if text == "{{":
            return "{"
        if text == "}}":
            return "}"
        if m.group(1) is not None:
            return values[m.group(1)]
        raise ValueError(f"unmatched brace at position {m.start()}")

    return token.sub(replace, template)
```

ways[j] counts embeddings of pattern[:j] in the processed source prefix; each source character matching pattern[j-1] adds ways[j-1], updated right-to-left so it is used once per sequence.
